# Chapter 7 — Motion

You have all three pieces now. Chapter 5 gave a thing a velocity and let the
tick carry it. Chapter 6 taught you to read the pad — to know, each frame,
whether a button is held. This chapter is where those two meet: a button decides
how a thing should move, and the heartbeat makes the move real. That is a game.

But before we wire the pad to a hero, there are two more ways to move a thing —
beyond the steady velocity of Chapter 5 — and each answers a different need. One
moves a thing *now*, this instant. The other gives it a *shove* the tick then
carries. Learn both, and you can express almost any motion a game needs.

## Move it now: `entity/move!`

Sometimes you do not want smooth drift — you want a thing *there*, immediately.
A teleport, a snap to a grid cell, a respawn. `entity/move!` shifts a thing by a
delta right away, no tick required:

```motoi
(require 'game/world)
(world/gravity! 0 0)
(define p (world/spawn 'p 10 10))
(entity/move! p 5 3)
(display (list (entity/x p) (entity/y p)))
(newline)
```

```
(15 13)
```

The thing started at `(10, 10)` and `entity/move!` slid it `5` right and `3`
down — instantly, before any `world/step`. That is the difference from velocity:
velocity is a *promise* the tick keeps over time, while `entity/move!` is a
change that happens the moment you call it. Reach for it when the motion should
be sudden, not gradual.

## Give it a shove: `entity/accel!`

Velocity from Chapter 5 was something you *set* — a fixed speed. But movement in
a game usually comes from a push: a thrust, a jump, a gust. `entity/accel!` is
that push. It **adds** to a thing's velocity, and then the tick carries the new,
faster motion forward:

```motoi
(require 'game/world)
(world/gravity! 0 0)
(define q (world/spawn 'q 0 0))
(entity/accel! q 2 0)
(world/step)
(world/step)
(display (list (entity/x q) (car (entity/vel q))))
(newline)
```

```
(4 2)
```

One shove of `2` gave `q` a velocity of `2`, and that velocity *stayed* — two
ticks later it has traveled `4` and is still moving `2` per frame. A shove is
not a nudge that fades; it is a lasting change to how the thing moves. Set a
velocity when you know the exact speed; **shove** when a force should add to
whatever motion is already there.

## A shove every frame is acceleration

Here is the idea that ties this chapter back to gravity. If one shove adds
speed, then a shove on *every* tick makes a thing speed up, faster and faster:

```motoi
(require 'game/world)
(world/gravity! 0 0)
(define r (world/spawn 'r 0 0))
(entity/accel! r 1 0)
(world/step)
(entity/accel! r 1 0)
(world/step)
(entity/accel! r 1 0)
(world/step)
(display (list (entity/x r) (car (entity/vel r))))
(newline)
```

```
(6 3)
```

Watch the distance: `1`, then `3`, then `6` — the very sequence you saw a drop
fall in Chapter 5. That is not a coincidence. **Gravity was a shove every
frame.** When you turned gravity on, the console was calling something like
`entity/accel!` for you, once per tick, in the down direction. Now you can be
that force yourself, in any direction — which is exactly what a thrust button
does.

## Wire the button to the shove

Now the synthesis. A steering routine reads the pad and shoves the hero
accordingly. In a live console you would run this every frame, and holding
`right` would push the hero right, tick after tick:

```motoi
(require 'game/world)
(world/gravity! 0 0)
(define hero (world/spawn 'hero 20 20))
(define (steer)
  (when (input/down? 'right) (entity/accel! hero 1 0))
  (when (input/down? 'left)  (entity/accel! hero -1 0)))
(define (push-right) (entity/accel! hero 4 0))
(push-right)
(world/step)
(display (entity/x hero))
(newline)
```

```
24
```

`steer` is the real pattern — *when `right` is down, shove right; when `left`
is down, shove left* — and it is the heart of every controllable character you
will build. At the prompt the pad is idle, so `steer` would shove nothing; to
watch the motion here we call `push-right`, the very shove that holding `right`
triggers, and the tick carries the hero from `20` to `24`. In a running console
the button calls it for you, sixty times a second, and the hero glides. You have
just built the loop that turns a press into movement.

## What you just proved

You added two motions to your vocabulary: `entity/move!`, the instant shift for
teleports and snaps, and `entity/accel!`, the shove that the tick carries and
that *stacks into acceleration* when repeated — the same force gravity applies.
Then you wired a button read to a shove and watched a press become movement.
Every program ran and reported real coordinates you could check.

This is the top of the mountain for motion: you can now place a thing, push it,
steer it, and let the heartbeat carry it all. The rest of the book adds the
other senses — first sound, so your moving world can be *heard*.

> **Tip**
>
> Three tools, three jobs: `entity/set-vel!` when you know the exact speed,
> `entity/accel!` when a force should add to existing motion, and
> `entity/move!` when the change should be instant. Most bugs where a character
> feels wrong are really the wrong tool — a teleport where you wanted a glide,
> or a set-velocity where a shove should have accumulated.

> **Warning**
>
> `entity/accel!` and velocity only take effect when the tick runs. A shove with
> no following `world/step` changes the thing's velocity but moves it nowhere —
> the position updates on the *next* heartbeat, not at the moment of the shove.
> If a pushed thing refuses to move, check that something is stepping the world.
> `entity/move!` is the exception: it shifts position immediately, tick or no
> tick.

